      html: "<p><span class='pnum'>1</span>The bakery woke before the street did. When Nena pushed through the back door at half past four, the ovens were already breathing, and her grandmother, whom everyone in the neighborhood called Tita, stood at the long steel table with flour to her elbows. She did not look up. She never looked up when the dough was at the stage she trusted least, the tender minute when it decided whether to become bread or ruin.</p><p><span class='pnum'>2</span>Nena hung her jacket on the nail by the sink and took her place across the table without being told. This was the arrangement they had kept for six years, since the winter Nena\u2019s mother moved north for work and left the two of them to manage the ovens and each other. They rarely spoke in those first hours. Tita believed that talk let the warmth out of a room, and that dough could hear.</p><p><span class='pnum'>3</span>For a while there was only the sound of their hands. Nena had learned the rhythm so completely that she could feel a mistake before she saw it: a batch mixed a shade too wet, a proof left a minute too long. Tita had taught her this the way she taught everything, by refusing to explain it. \u201cYou will know,\u201d she had said, the first morning, when Nena asked how much flour was enough. \u201cThe day you stop asking me, you will know.\u201d</p><p><span class='pnum'>4</span>By six the first customers would come, and they came for Tita as much as for the bread. The retired mailman who wanted the heel of the day-old loaf because his teeth were bad and his pride was worse; the twins from the third floor who paid in coins warm from their pockets; the nurse coming off her shift who never spoke but touched the counter twice, a habit Tita returned without ever mentioning. Nena had grown up believing the shop was a kind of engine that kept the whole block running, and that her grandmother was the thing at its center that must never be allowed to stop.</p><p><span class='pnum'>5</span>What Tita did not know was that an envelope had been sitting in Nena\u2019s jacket for eleven days, folded once against the lining. It carried the seal of an art school in the city, three hundred miles south, and inside it a single word that had rearranged everything: admitted. Nena had read it so many times the crease had gone soft as cloth. She had not yet found the shape of the sentence that would tell her grandmother she meant to go.</p><p><span class='pnum'>6</span>\u201cYou are quiet even for you,\u201d Tita said at last, sliding a tray toward the oven. It was not a question, and Nena did not answer it. Instead she watched the older woman\u2019s shoulders, which had a language of their own. When Tita was angry they climbed toward her ears. This morning they stayed low, careful, as if she were carrying something she did not want to spill.</p><p><span class='pnum'>7</span>The first loaves came out too dark. Tita set them on the rack without a word and stood looking at them a long moment. Six years, and Nena had never seen her burn a batch. \u201cThe oven runs hot today,\u201d Nena offered, though the oven ran exactly as it always did. Tita nodded slowly, accepting the kindness of the lie, and Nena understood then that her grandmother already knew about the envelope, had perhaps known for days, and was waiting, as she waited for dough, to see what it would become.</p><p><span class='pnum'>8</span>When the light finally came up gray in the front windows, Tita wiped her hands and reached under the counter for the tin where she kept the things she could not throw away: a photograph, a child\u2019s paper crown, a pressed flower the color of weak tea. She took out a folded square of paper and pressed it flat on the table. It was a drawing Nena had made at seven, a lopsided loaf with a face, grinning. \u201cYou signed it,\u201d Tita said. \u201cIn the corner. Even then you wanted your name on what you made.\u201d</p><p><span class='pnum'>9</span>She did not say go, and she did not say stay. She slid the drawing back into the tin and set a fresh bowl between them, and the flour rose between their hands like a small weather. But she had said the thing under the thing, and Nena, who had learned the whole language of that kitchen without a single lesson she could name, folded the next loaf and felt the envelope grow lighter in the other room, as if some of its weight had finally been set down.</p>",
