      title: "Passage II — SOCIAL SCIENCE",
      intro: "This passage is adapted from \u201cThe Clock in the Town Square\u201d by Miriam Estrada, a history of how ordinary people came to share the same time.",
      html: "<p><span class='pnum'>1</span>For a few strange years, the railway station in Millbrook kept two clocks on the same wall. One, painted with the town seal, showed the hour the town had always trusted, set each week against the sun. The other, plainer and slightly ahead, showed what the railroad called standard time. To later travelers the pairing looked quaint. To the people who lived through it, those two clocks marked the moment when time quietly stopped belonging to the town.</p><p><span class='pnum'>2</span>Before uniform time, each community set its own. Noon arrived when the sun stood highest overhead, and because that moment differs by a few minutes from one place to the next, a clock in one town might read several minutes apart from a clock fifty miles away. No one minded. Time was local and sensory, tied to the length of a shadow, the ringing of a bell, the closing of a shop. A traveler crossing the countryside adjusted a pocket watch the way one adjusts to a change in weather. In most places the duty of keeping time fell to a single trusted person\u2014a jeweler, perhaps, or the keeper of the town clock\u2014who checked the hour against the sun and rang or posted it for everyone else. Time was something a community produced for itself, out in the open, and could point to overhead at noon.</p><p><span class='pnum'>3</span>Railroads could not run on such a patchwork. A line passing through a dozen towns faced a dozen slightly different noons, and a timetable printed in one town became a small riddle in the next. Worse, the confusion was dangerous. Two trains sharing a single track relied on schedules measured to the minute, and a minute of disagreement could put them on the same rail at the same instant. Dispatchers grew used to a low, constant dread, and every near miss sharpened the case for reform. The pressure for a single, agreed-upon time did not come from philosophers. It came from the need to keep engines from meeting head-on.</p><p><span class='pnum'>4</span>The reform, when it arrived, was startling in its speed. A committee of railway managers divided the country into broad bands, each keeping one time, and chose a day on which every station would change at once. When that day came, telegraph signals raced along the wires, and clerks across the region reached up and moved the hands of their station clocks to match. In a single afternoon, thousands of clocks that had always disagreed were made to agree.</p><p><span class='pnum'>5</span>Not everyone welcomed the change. Newspapers printed indignant letters, and from more than one pulpit came the charge that it was arrogance to override the sun\u2019s testimony, to let a railroad decree when noon should fall. A few towns kept their old \u201ctown time\u201d on a second clock for years, a small rebellion against the idea that a distant office could tell them what hour it was. Farmers grumbled that the new noon no longer matched the sun in their own fields, and more than one editor asked by what right a company could revise the heavens. The complaint was not really about minutes. It was about who held the authority to name the day.</p><p><span class='pnum'>6</span>Yet the new time won, and it won for reasons that had little to do with argument. Factories opened and closed by the timetable. Markets, mails, and meetings arranged themselves around the trains. A merchant who insisted on local time simply missed his shipments. Against this steady economic gravity, sentiment could not hold. Schools set their bells by it, newspapers printed the standard hour without comment, and children grew up never knowing there had been another way. Within a generation the second clock came down, and the standard hour became so ordinary that people forgot it had ever been imposed.</p><p><span class='pnum'>7</span>That forgetting is the part worth remembering. The deeper change was not mechanical but a shift in ownership. Time had been drawn from the sky and the parish bell, things a community could see and hear for itself. Now it flowed from a schedule fixed elsewhere, delivered by wire, accepted on trust. We gained a genuine convenience, and it is hard to imagine modern life without it. But we also handed something over, quietly and almost gratefully, and most of us have never noticed the trade.</p>",
