      html: "<p><span class='pnum'>1</span>Walk into an old forest and it is easy to see the trees as separate individuals, each competing with its neighbors for light and soil. Below the surface, however, a different picture emerges. The roots of most forest trees are wrapped in fine fungal threads, and through those threads the trees are joined to one another in a web so extensive that a single teaspoon of soil may hold miles of it. The forest, it turns out, is far more connected than it looks. Biologists have a name for these root-and-fungus partnerships\u2014mycorrhizae, from the Greek words for fungus and root\u2014and they are not a rare curiosity but the ordinary condition of the forest. The great majority of land plants form them, and have done so for hundreds of millions of years.</p><p><span class='pnum'>2</span>The partnership at the heart of this web is an exchange. Fungi cannot make their own food, but they are superb miners. Their threads, called hyphae, spread far beyond the reach of any root, gathering scarce nutrients such as phosphorus and nitrogen, along with water, and delivering them to the tree. In return the tree pays the fungi in sugars, the products of photosynthesis that the fungi cannot manufacture for themselves. Each partner supplies what the other lacks. It is a trade as old as forests. Neither partner is doing the other a favor; each simply cannot thrive alone, and natural selection has bound their fortunes together.</p><p><span class='pnum'>3</span>Because a single fungus can connect to many trees at once, these partnerships link individuals into what researchers call a common mycorrhizal network. Along its threads travel not only nutrients but signals, and\u2014most surprising of all\u2014carbon, the very currency of a plant\u2019s energy. A network does not stop at the base of one trunk. It reaches outward, tree to tree, weaving a stand of separate plants into something that behaves, at least in part, as a shared system. The scale can be startling. A single fungal individual may spread through a patch of forest larger than a city block, touching dozens of trees, so that a nutrient absorbed at one edge might surface in a tree far away.</p><p><span class='pnum'>4</span>The most striking evidence came from a clever experiment. Researchers enclosed the leaves of tall, well-lit trees in chambers filled with carbon dioxide made from a rare, traceable form of carbon. Weeks later they searched for that distinctive label elsewhere in the forest. They found it in the tissues of small, shaded seedlings growing nearby\u2014seedlings that had never touched those chambers. The labeled carbon had traveled underground, from trees with plenty of light to seedlings struggling in the shade, passing through the fungal web between them. The distance the carbon covered ruled out any simple explanation of soil drift; the fungal threads were the only bridge that fit.</p><p><span class='pnum'>5</span>What such a finding means is still debated. Some scientists read it as cooperation, a forest sharing its wealth so that its youngest members survive. Others urge caution, noting that the fungus, not the tree, controls much of the flow, and may be moving carbon to serve its own ends rather than out of any generosity between trees. The label proves that carbon moves. It does not, by itself, prove why.</p><p><span class='pnum'>6</span>The very word market, useful as it is, carries its own risk. It invites us to imagine trees as traders weighing costs and benefits, when in fact no tree decides anything. A comparison can illuminate a process and still mislead us if we forget it is a comparison. The exchange is real; the negotiating shopkeepers are a picture in our heads.</p><p><span class='pnum'>7</span>Still, the discovery reshapes how we see a woodland. A forest is not only a crowd of competitors but also a fabric of connections, and to cut one tree may be to sever links that reach far beyond its own roots. Foresters and conservationists have begun to take the network seriously, treating the soil not as a passive backdrop but as a living system whose threads are worth protecting. Some now argue that when a forest is logged, the oldest and most connected trees should be spared as anchors of the network, hubs from which recovery can spread. Whether or not that hope proves out, the underground web has already changed the questions worth asking about a forest.</p>",
