      intro: "This passage is adapted from \u201cDrawn by Hand\u201d by Idris Bello, an essay on animation.",
      html: "<p><span class='pnum'>1</span>Every few years someone announces that hand-drawn animation is finished, a relic overtaken by the smooth precision of computers. The obituary is always premature. It rests on a mistaken belief that a newer method must simply replace an older one, the way a faster engine replaces a slower one. But an art form is not an engine. Hand-drawing is not an inferior way of doing what computer animation does. It is a different thing altogether, and I mean to explain what would be lost if we let it disappear. The confusion is understandable. In most technologies the new really does retire the old; few people pine for the telegraph now that we have the telephone. But tools that make things and tools that make art do not obey the same law. A painter\u2019s acrylics did not abolish oil paint, and the photograph did not end drawing. Each new means adds a voice; it does not silence the others.</p><p><span class='pnum'>2</span>Consider what the hand actually gives. A line drawn by a person carries the small tremors and hesitations of the body that made it, the record of a thousand tiny decisions about pressure and speed. Watch an old animated figure and you can feel those decisions; the line seems to breathe, alive with the faint imperfection of having been made by a human being. That imperfection is not a flaw to be corrected. It is the very thing the eye responds to, the fingerprint of a maker on every frame. Perfection, oddly, can feel colder; a line too clean can look as though no one ever lived inside it.</p><p><span class='pnum'>3</span>None of this is meant to scorn the computer, which can do wonders no pencil could attempt. Studios did not abandon the drawing board out of malice; they did it because computer animation was faster and cheaper to produce, and in an industry ruled by budgets, efficiency wins arguments. Whole departments of artists were retired, and for a time it seemed the pencil had lost for good. I understand the arithmetic. I only insist that arithmetic is not the whole story. A thing can be cheaper to make and poorer to receive, and a culture that counts only the first number will keep making that trade until it forgets there was ever a choice.</p><p><span class='pnum'>4</span>For audiences never stopped responding to the hand-drawn image. At film festivals, small studios working in the old way draw crowds who sense, even without knowing why, that they are watching something warmer than the polished blockbusters down the hall. Independent films made frame by frame have found devoted followings, and animators trained on computers increasingly seek out the pencil for the qualities it alone provides. The demand did not die. It went quiet, and now it is loud again. Streaming services advertise their hand-drawn features as prestige pieces, and a new generation of students crowds into the few programs that still teach the craft. The market, having declared the pencil dead, keeps quietly reviving it whenever it wants an audience to feel something.</p><p><span class='pnum'>5</span>My argument, then, is not nostalgia. I am not asking anyone to prefer the old simply because it is old. I am making a claim about expression: that certain feelings reach us most directly through the trace of a human hand, and that a technology able to imitate that trace is not the same as the thing itself. A machine can copy the wobble of a hand-drawn line. It cannot copy the choosing that produced it, the labor and judgment folded into every stroke. This is not a mystical claim. It is the same reason a handwritten letter can move us more than the identical words in print: we sense the person behind the marks. Take that person away and something specific, and irreplaceable, goes with them.</p><p><span class='pnum'>6</span>This is why I resist calling hand-drawing a stage of progress, a rung we have climbed past on the way to something better. It is better to call it a language\u2014one of several that animators may speak, each capable of saying things the others cannot. We do not retire a language because a newer one exists. We would be poorer for the silence. The pencil is not the past of animation. It is one of its living voices, and a wise art keeps all its voices.</p>",
