      html: "<p><span class='pnum'>1</span>A glacier looks like the very image of stillness, a frozen thing where nothing happens. In fact it is one of the most patient record keepers on Earth. Snow that falls on a high ice sheet does not simply melt away; layer settles upon layer, year after year, and the deep ice becomes an archive, a preserved record of the skies that made it. To a scientist willing to drill and to read, a column of ice is a library of ancient weather. The idea took a while to be believed. When researchers first proposed that gases sealed in glacier ice could reveal the makeup of long-vanished skies, the claim struck many as too good to be true. Decades of patient work have since turned that hope into one of the sturdiest tools in the study of climate.</p><p><span class='pnum'>2</span>The reading begins with what the ice traps. When snow falls, it is fluffy and full of air, and as fresh snow piles on top, the older layers are squeezed until the spaces between the flakes pinch shut. Bubbles of the old atmosphere are sealed inside, tiny pockets of air from the year the snow fell. Millennia later, a researcher can crush a sliver of that ice and release a breath of an ancient sky, then measure the gases it contains. Few other records preserve the actual air of the distant past.</p><p><span class='pnum'>3</span>The layers themselves can be read like the rings of a tree. In many cores the annual bands are visible, a light summer layer above a darker winter one, and counting them measures the passage of years. Bands of fine dust mark seasons of drought or distant volcanic eruptions. Subtler still, the ice records temperature: the two forms, or isotopes, of oxygen locked in the water molecules occur in slightly different proportions depending on how cold it was when the snow formed, so the ice itself becomes a frozen thermometer.</p><p><span class='pnum'>4</span>A recent project shows how far the reading can reach. Drilling at a remote site, a team led by the glaciologist Anders Holt recovered a core more than two miles long, its deepest ice laid down some hundred and twenty thousand years ago. Extracting it took years of work in brutal cold, and analyzing it took longer still, but the reward was a continuous record of the atmosphere spanning more than a hundred millennia, from a warm age before the last great ice advance down to the present. Each section of that core had to be kept frozen and uncontaminated on its long journey to the laboratory, for a single careless thaw could blur the very record the team had traveled so far to read.</p><p><span class='pnum'>5</span>What the core revealed was a striking partnership. Across that long span, the amount of carbon dioxide in the trapped air and the temperature written in the isotopes rose and fell together, tracking each other so closely that a graph of one nearly traces the other. The record also held surprises: intervals when the climate lurched from one state to another not over thousands of years but within a few decades, far faster than anyone had once thought possible.</p><p><span class='pnum'>6</span>None of this should be read without caution. A single core speaks most reliably for the place where it was drilled, and knitting many cores into a global picture is careful work. The deeper the ice, the more it has been squeezed and stirred, and the harder it is to date each layer exactly. Good science treats an ice core as a powerful witness whose testimony must still be weighed, not as an oracle that settles every question on its own. Comparing several cores from different regions, and checking the ice against other records such as ocean sediments and ancient tree rings, is how a local story is turned into a global one.</p><p><span class='pnum'>7</span>Yet the value of that testimony is hard to overstate. To judge how unusual the present may be, we need a long view of what came before, and the ice supplies it. Locked in those quiet layers is the closest thing we have to a diary of the planet\u2019s breathing, and learning to read it is among the surer ways of understanding the changes unfolding around us now. The ice cannot tell us what to do. It can only tell us, with unusual clarity, where we stand.</p>",
