      intro: "This passage is adapted from the short story \u201cThe Tuning Note\u201d by Yusuf Halloran.",
      html: "<p><span class='pnum'>1</span>The A came out sharp, and Mara turned the peg a hair\u2019s width until the note settled into the one the oboe had given her, the note the whole orchestra would build itself upon. Backstage smelled of dust and old velvet. She had tuned this same A ten thousand times, and it had always been only a beginning; tonight it felt like the edge of a small cliff. Beyond the curtain an audience she could not see was finding its seats, and her hands, which had never once failed her in the practice room, had begun to feel like objects she was only borrowing.</p><p><span class='pnum'>2</span>Her mother sat in the corner on a folding chair, coat still buttoned, hands folded in her lap as though she were holding something that might spill. Renata had a gift for stillness. In a room full of pacing musicians and murmured warm-ups she could sit so quietly that people forgot she was there, and Mara had learned to read that stillness the way sailors read a sky\u2014not as emptiness but as weather held in reserve.</p><p><span class='pnum'>3</span>At home there was a cello case in the hall closet, behind the winter coats, and in sixteen years Mara had never once heard it opened. She had known since she was small that the case was not a thing to ask about. The knowledge had come to her the way such rules come to children, without a word ever being spoken: a door that was simply understood to stay shut. Sometimes, reaching past it for a scarf, she had let her hand rest on the case\u2019s cold latch and felt the shape of a question she had no permission to ask.</p><p><span class='pnum'>4</span>\u201cYou drifted sharp on the tuning,\u201d Renata said now, not unkindly. \u201cYour ear is fine. It is your hands that are nervous. Let them be nervous and play anyway; the nerves will follow the music once you begin.\u201d Her voice was low and even, the voice of someone reporting the weather, and it steadied Mara more than any softer comfort could have.</p><p><span class='pnum'>5</span>It was Renata who had given her the first notes, years ago, guiding her small fingers on a half-size violin and singing the pitches when Mara could not find them. Then, without explanation, the lessons had stopped. A teacher was found, a good one, and her mother stepped back to the edge of the music and stayed there\u2014present at every recital, silent on every drive home, a listener so attentive that her silence seemed to have a shape. As a girl Mara had assumed that every mother did this, hovered at the doorway of a thing she loved. Only later did she begin to wonder what it might cost to stand so near a fire and never once warm your hands.</p><p><span class='pnum'>6</span>Renata rose and crossed to her. She did not offer advice. She reached out and turned down the corner of Mara\u2019s collar, which had folded under, and pressed a fresh cake of rosin into her palm, closing her daughter\u2019s fingers around it. The gesture was so ordinary and so careful that Mara felt her throat tighten, and she looked hard at the floor until the feeling passed.</p><p><span class='pnum'>7</span>\u201cWhy did you stop,\u201d Mara said. She had not planned to ask; the question simply arrived. For a moment her mother\u2019s face did something complicated. \u201cThere was you, and there was work, and there was only so much of me,\u201d Renata said at last. \u201cIt is an old story and not a sad one.\u201d But her hand had come to rest on her own forearm, on the spot where a player draws a cello close, and Mara understood that the story was at least a little sad, and that her mother had decided long ago not to let the sadness be seen. She waited, but her mother offered nothing more.</p><p><span class='pnum'>8</span>A woman with a clipboard opened the door and said Mara\u2019s name. Renata stepped back. \u201cDo not play for them,\u201d she said quietly. \u201cThey are only walls and chairs. Play for the music, and let them listen in.\u201d It was the most she had said about music in years, and Mara took it with her like a coin pressed into the hand.</p><p><span class='pnum'>9</span>On the stage the lights turned the audience into a single dark shape, and for one breath Mara was afraid. Then she set the bow to the string and drew the first long note, and it came out steady, steadier than her hands had any right to make it. Somewhere behind her, in the wings, she knew her mother was standing very still, listening, and Mara played the phrase her mother had never let herself play, and gave it back to her across the dark.</p>",
