      title: "Passage II — SOCIAL SCIENCE",
      intro: "This passage is adapted from \u201cThe Well-Tended Commons\u201d by Delphine Marchetti, an anthropologist who studies how communities manage shared resources.",
      html: "<p><span class='pnum'>1</span>Picture a pasture open to all. Each herder who grazes an animal there gains the full value of that animal, while the cost of the added grazing\u2014the slow thinning of the grass\u2014is spread among everyone. The logic seems to lead in one grim direction: each herder, acting sensibly, keeps adding animals until the pasture is stripped bare and the shared resource collapses. This parable of inevitable ruin has become one of the most quoted ideas in the social sciences. It is vivid, memorable, and, taken as a complete account of human behavior, wrong.</p><p><span class='pnum'>2</span>The parable assumes that the herders can only watch helplessly as disaster approaches, unable to speak to one another or agree on limits. Real communities are rarely so mute. For as long as people have shared meadows, forests, and fishing grounds, they have also invented ways to govern them, and the historical record is crowded with commons that were used for centuries without being destroyed. To notice this is not to deny that resources can be ruined. It is to ask a sharper question: why are some ruined and others not?</p><p><span class='pnum'>3</span>Consider the high villages of one alpine valley, where families held their private hayfields in the lowlands but grazed their cattle together on the common summer meadows above. For more than five hundred years these pastures were governed by written rules that the villagers themselves revised in yearly assemblies. A household could send to the high meadow only as many cattle as it could feed on its own land through the winter\u2014a clever limit that tied the right to use the commons to the burden of sustaining the herd the rest of the year. A common herdsman, chosen by the villagers and paid in cheese and grain, drove the united herd to the heights each June and answered to the assembly if the count came back wrong. The rule was simple enough for anyone to check and hard for anyone to evade, and that plainness was its strength.</p><p><span class='pnum'>4</span>Such arrangements shared a set of features that scholars now recognize as the marks of a durable commons. The people who used the resource, not a distant authority, made the rules. Boundaries were clear, so everyone knew who was entitled to what. Members watched one another, and because they lived side by side, cheating was hard to hide. Penalties for breaking the rules were real but graduated: a small fine for a first offense, sterner measures only for the stubborn. Fairness, visibility, and trust did the work that fences and hired guards could not.</p><p><span class='pnum'>5</span>Local control mattered because the people on the ground knew things no outsider could. They knew which slope recovered slowly after a dry summer, which stream could turn one more mill, how far the herd could grow before the grass began to fail. Rules written far away, however well intended, tended to miss these particulars, and rules imposed without consent were obeyed grudgingly if at all. The commons worked best when the governed and the governors were the same people. A farmer who would have to live beside his neighbors for the rest of his life, and whose children would inherit the same fields, had every reason to weigh the meadow\u2019s long future against a season\u2019s quick gain.</p><p><span class='pnum'>6</span>None of this should be mistaken for a romance of the simple village. Commons did fail, and often. When a population grew too fast, when a distant market abruptly made it profitable to strip a forest bare, when war or new law dissolved the old assemblies, local rules could be swept away within a single generation. The point is not that communities always succeed. It is that they sometimes do, and that their successes follow a pattern we can actually study.</p><p><span class='pnum'>7</span>That pattern carries a lesson our own age needs. Faced with a shared resource under strain\u2014a fishery, an aquifer, the very air we breathe\u2014we tend to reach for one of two remedies: hand the resource to a single private owner, or hand it to the state. These histories suggest a third possibility, older and easily overlooked. Where the users can talk, make their own rules, and hold one another to them, ruin is not a law of nature. It is one outcome among several, and the institutions people build are what tip the balance.</p>",
