      html: "<div class='sub-passage'><h4>Passage A — by <em>Ingrid Sollazzo</em></h4><p><span class='pnum'>1</span>Each autumn, small songbirds that have never made the journey before fly thousands of miles to wintering grounds they have never seen. A young warbler, raised in a northern forest and setting out alone after its parents have already gone, somehow holds a steady course toward a destination encoded in no map it was taught. For a long time this feat seemed almost miraculous. We now know that much of it rests on an ability the birds carry within their own bodies: a magnetic compass.</p><p><span class='pnum'>2</span>The clearest evidence comes from the orientation cage, a simple funnel in which a captive migratory bird, restless to depart, hops and flutters toward the side that faces its natural migratory direction, leaving a record of scratches. When researchers surround such a cage with coils that reshape the magnetic field passing through it, the birds shift their preferred direction to match. Turn the artificial field, and the birds turn with it. Their sense of which way to go tracks the magnetic field, not the walls of the room or the position of the sun.</p><p><span class='pnum'>3</span>Stranger still, this compass does not seem to work like the needle in a hiker\u2019s hand. That familiar device points along the field toward the magnetic pole. The bird\u2019s compass instead appears to read the angle at which the field lines dip into the earth, distinguishing \u201ctoward the pole\u201d from \u201ctoward the equator\u201d rather than north from south. Reverse the horizontal direction of the field alone and the birds are unmoved; alter the dip of the lines and their behavior changes at once. This inclination compass, as researchers call it, would explain why a bird carried across the equator can grow confused, its sense of \u201cpoleward\u201d suddenly pointing the opposite way.</p><p><span class='pnum'>4</span>How a living animal senses something as faint as the earth\u2019s magnetism remains partly open, but a leading idea points to the eye. Certain proteins in the retina, when struck by light, may enter states whose chemistry is subtly swayed by a magnetic field, so that the bird could in effect see its heading as a pattern laid over its visual world. Fittingly, the compass falters in total darkness and under certain colors of light, as if it needed illumination to function. Whatever its exact machinery, the magnetic compass gives the young bird what it most needs on its first flight: a dependable sense of direction it was born already knowing.</p></div><div class='sub-passage'><h4>Passage B — by <em>Theo Nakamura</em></h4><p><span class='pnum'>1</span>It is tempting, once a magnetic compass has been demonstrated in migrating birds, to conclude that the puzzle of navigation is solved. I want to resist that temptation. A bird crossing a continent is not consulting a single instrument. It is weighing a whole committee of cues, and the magnetic field, for all its elegance, is only one voice among them.</p><p><span class='pnum'>2</span>Consider the stars. Raise young songbirds under a planetarium sky and they learn to read the rotation of the heavens, fixing on the still point around which the night sky turns and using it to find their bearing. Deny them that view and their star sense never develops properly. Here is a compass that must be learned from the world, not merely inherited, and it operates entirely without reference to magnetism. The lesson is that at least one of the bird\u2019s compasses is not a birthright at all but a skill acquired by watching the sky wheel overhead, night after night, through a single formative season.</p><p><span class='pnum'>3</span>The setting sun matters too. Many migrants take a reading from the glow of dusk and the polarized light that fans across the sky at that hour, and experiments suggest they use it to correct their other compasses before a night of flying. A bird that has watched the sun go down departs better oriented than one denied the view. Landmarks\u2014a coastline, a river valley, a mountain ridge\u2014offer still another layer of guidance, and some evidence hints that smell contributes as well. No one of these cues is flawless. Clouds can hide the stars, an overcast dusk can swallow the polarized pattern, and a magnetic anomaly in the rocks below can nudge the compass astray. A bird that leaned on any single sense would be lost the first time that sense failed it.</p><p><span class='pnum'>4</span>What impresses me is not any one of these senses but the way birds weave them together and check one against another. When cues are made to disagree in the laboratory, birds do not cling stubbornly to the magnetic field; often they recalibrate, letting the setting sun or the stars adjust what the compass tells them. A traveler with several imperfect instruments, each prone to error in different conditions, is wise to consult them all. To credit the magnetic sense alone is to mistake one member of the committee for the whole.</p></div>",
